cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use context-contamination-probe in research or software, please cite it as below."
title: "context-contamination-probe"
type: software
authors:
  - family-names: Dziatkovskii
    given-names: Anton
    orcid: "https://orcid.org/0000-0001-7408-3054"
    affiliation: "Palo Alto AI Research Lab"
repository-code: "https://github.com/tonydzi/context-contamination-probe"
url: "https://github.com/tonydzi/context-contamination-probe"
license: MIT
abstract: >-
  A single-file, stdlib-only probe that asks each agent CLI what it can see in its own
  context, and diffs the answers across runtimes. Agent CLIs load instruction files
  implicitly and asymmetrically - Claude Code takes CLAUDE.md, MEMORY.md and its skill
  list, OpenAI Codex CLI walks up from the working directory collecting AGENTS.md plus
  CODEX_HOME, xAI Grok CLI reads its global config with no off switch, Google Gemini CLI
  takes only its own scaffolding - so any cross-runtime comparison measures the layers
  before it measures the models. Summing bytes on disk gives an upper bound, not a fact,
  because it cannot say which layers a runtime actually admitted into the window after
  hierarchical lookups and imports. The probe sends one identical enumeration question
  per rail, greps the answers for markers of a personal layer, and refuses to score
  non-answers as clean: an empty reply, a crash, a revoked token and a refusal to
  disclose each get their own verdict and exit code rather than collapsing into a pass.
  Canary mode substitutes unique tokens for guesswork when the question is which specific
  file reached the window. Four rails are wired; the 54-test grid runs with no LLM calls
  and no network.
keywords:
  - agent-cli
  - context-window
  - prompt-contamination
  - benchmark-hygiene
  - claude-code
  - codex-cli
  - reproducibility
